 - A tool call a model writes into its reply's text, when the request offered tools, is made the call it is ([`textcalls.go`](../../internal/gateway/textcalls.go), #823): Hermes' `<tool_call>{…}</tool_call>`, DeepSeek's DSML (`<｜DSML｜invoke name=…>` with its parameters), DeepSeek's half-Hermes `<parameter>` form (#917) and GLM's `<arg_key>`/`<arg_value>` (#906), read by `parseTextCalls`. Each block must name an offered tool and have arguments that can be read; else the text goes on as it came. The text before the first block is kept, what is left of the tags goes, and the reply ends as one that called tools (`finish_reason` `tool_calls`, `stop_reason` `tool_use`). A reply whose model made a call itself is left alone. Every route reads it the same way: a relayed Chat stream (`textCallTidy`), a relayed Chat reply that came whole, JSON or under `text/plain` or no type (`chatWhole` with `wholeTextCalls`, #1845: a `stream:false` request kept the markup and `finish_reason` `stop`), which also covers a vendor that answered a streamed request whole (`wholeAsStream`), and every translation, which magpie always asks streamed, a plugin's included, whatever the client asked (`textCallSee`). A relayed Responses or Messages upstream's text is not read for calls. Covered by `TestTextWritten*`, `TestGLMTextCall*`, `TestPluginTextWrittenCall` and `TestQuietWholeReplyStreamed`.
 - Structured output is carried across every translation (`Format`, [`format.go`](../../internal/gateway/format.go)). It is read from Responses' `text.format`, Chat's `response_format`, Anthropic's `output_config.format` and Gemini's `responseMimeType` with `responseJsonSchema`/`responseSchema`: `json_schema` with its name, description, schema and strict, or `json_object`. Each upstream is asked in its own field: `text.format` on Responses, `response_format` on Chat, `output_config.format` on Messages, and `generationConfig.responseMimeType`/`responseJsonSchema` on Gemini and Code Assist. An API with no name for it gets `response`. Messages and the Claude subscription's `--json-schema` take a schema only, so `json_object` is told in the system prompt there (`inSystem`). The bridges to a vendor's own API (Cursor, Devin, Kiro, Qoder, Command Code, Zed) are always told in the system prompt. A 400 or 422 that names the field (`refusesFormat`: DeepSeek's "This response_format type is unavailable now", a schema Anthropic can't hold) marks the provider and model (`formatRefused`), and the request is asked again with the format told in the system prompt, as are later requests to that provider and model. A relayed request keeps its format as sent. A Gemini client's format is also still told in its system prompt, as before. A model may still wrap its JSON answer in a Markdown code fence; a client that asked for structured output, in a field or told in the system prompt (`wantsJSON`), gets the JSON inside a single unlabelled or json-labelled fence when that content is a complete valid JSON value, streamed (`fenceEncoder`) or whole (`unfencedResult`), in [`format_fence.go`](../../internal/gateway/format_fence.go). Invalid or truncated JSON, other fence languages, text outside the fence, and answers to plain-text clients reach the client as they came. This normalization applies to translated replies; native protocol passthrough stays byte-preserving.
